  "narrative": "<h3 class=\"story-subheading\">Short impact summary</h3>\n<p class=\"story-text\">This 2021 scholarly book is already showing early academic uptake, with 3 citations in OpenAlex and 3,415 recorded downloads through OAPEN. Its strongest signal is practical and policy relevance: the volume addresses translation policies in legal and institutional settings, aligning with Peace, Justice and strong institutions and drawing on a clearly interdisciplinary frame.</p>\n<h3 class=\"story-subheading\">Full narrative</h3>\n<p class=\"story-text\">Translation Policies in Legal and Institutional Settings brings together case studies from different parts of the world to examine how translation policy is shaped in legal and institutional contexts. The book\u2019s impact record suggests that its value lies in bridging scholarship and policy-facing questions: it has attracted early citations, sustained open-access readership, and funding support from KU Leuven and CETRA. Together, these signals point to a work that is being used as a reference point for research on multilingual governance, legal translation, and institutional language practice.</p>\n<p class=\"story-text\">The citation profile is still modest, but it is meaningful as an early indication of scholarly attention. The citing works appear in translation-studies journals and span topics such as legal translation studies, translation history, and the Chinese-English translation of legal texts. That pattern suggests the book is resonating within a focused specialist community rather than across a broad general readership, which is consistent with its subject matter and edited, research-oriented design.</p>\n<p class=\"story-text\">Usage data provide a stronger signal than citation counts at this stage. More than three thousand downloads through OAPEN indicate substantial reader interest and continued accessibility. Because the book is available in open form, those downloads are a useful marker of reach in libraries, classrooms, and among researchers working at the intersection of translation studies, law, and multilingual policy.</p>\n<p class=\"story-text\">The conceptual profile reinforces this interpretation. The book is associated with citizenship, multilingualism, legal translation, political science, and translation studies, showing a strong policy and governance orientation. Its SDG alignment with Peace, Justice and strong institutions further underscores its relevance to debates about institutional language rights, access, and fairness in legal and public settings. Overall, the record shows a work that is early in its citation trajectory but already well positioned as a resource for both scholarship and policy-relevant discussion.</p>",
